I'm pretty sure that I do. 
Here is how Netty is integrated into our project.

``` java
bootstrap = new ServerBootstrap(new NioServerSocketChannelFactory(Executors.newCachedThreadPool(), executor, threadsCount));

// Set up the event pipeline factory.
bootstrap.setPipelineFactory(new ChannelPipelineFactory() {
    @Override
    public ChannelPipeline getPipeline() throws Exception {
        ChannelPipeline pipeline = Channels.pipeline();
        pipeline.addLast("decoder", new HttpRequestDecoder(8192, 8192, 8192)); 
        pipeline.addLast("aggregator", new HttpChunkAggregator(1048576));
        pipeline.addLast("encoder", new HttpResponseEncoder());
        pipeline.addLast("handler", new MySimpleChannelUpstreamHandler(.....));
        return pipeline;
    }
});
bootstrap.setOption("backlog", backlog);        
bootstrap.bind(address);
```

There is another thread that monitor channels activity and close inactive channels

``` java
public void run() { 
...
Channel expired = ..... get channel with no activity for 4 sec
if (expired!=null) expired.close();
...
}
```

this "expired.close()" call triggers NPE and seems to move HttpMessageDecoder into invalid state - another exception can follow right after original NPE

Could you see if you replace Channel.close() with the following it still gives issues:

Channel.write(ChannelBuffers.EMTPY_BUFFER).addListener(ChannelFutureListener.CLOSE);

Thanks...
